// Practice quiz: How transactions work in Spring
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/relationships-and-transactions/how-transactions-work-in-spring

::o1 placement::Where does @Transactional usually belong? {
=On the service method that represents one business operation#Correct. It groups that operation writes.
~On the entity class#Not quite. Entities map data; they do not start transactions.
~On each record#Not quite. Records are not the unit of work.
~On the main class#Not quite. That is too broad.
}

::o2 unchecked::A @Transactional method throws a RuntimeException. What is the default outcome? {
=Rollback#Correct. Unchecked exceptions roll back by default.
~Commit#Not quite. That is the default for checked exceptions.
~The exception is swallowed#Not quite. It still propagates.
~Retry#Not quite. There is no automatic retry.
}

::o2 checked::A @Transactional method throws a checked exception, with no rollback settings. What is the default outcome? {
=Commit#Correct. Checked exceptions do not roll back by default.
~Rollback#Not quite. It requires rollbackFor.
~The JVM exits#Not quite. It does not.
~A compile error#Not quite. It compiles normally.
}

::o2 self invocation::Why does this.other() not start a transaction for a @Transactional method? {
=The call does not pass through the proxy#Correct. The proxy applies the behavior.
~Private fields block it#Not quite. Fields are unrelated.
~this cannot call public methods#Not quite. It can.
~Spring forbids this calls#Not quite. It simply does not intercept them.
}

::o3 read only::What does readOnly \= true express? {
=A read path, which allows optimizations such as skipping dirty checks#Correct. It states the intent of the method.
~It locks the row#Not quite. It does not lock rows.
~It caches results forever#Not quite. It does not cache.
~It blocks other transactions#Not quite. It does not.
}

